Keep the public random generator's state per thread

Applies to picoquic 70b5fd5aafdd2d6c349ff783202ece9d778bcaf2, after 0001 to
0006.

picoquic_public_random_64() and the functions built on it draw from one
xorshift1024* state shared by the whole process, with no synchronization:
path challenges, the spin bit policy of a new connection, sequence number
holes, stateless reset padding, version negotiation, and the placeholder
reset secret of a new remote connection ID all go through it. A process
that runs several QUIC contexts on several threads, each context used from
one thread, races on that state: ThreadSanitizer reports every draw, and
between the index's increment and its mask the stored index is one past the
array, so another thread's draw can read seed[16].

The state is now per thread (PTLS_THREADLOCAL, as picotls keeps its own
per-thread state). A thread's state is seeded from the crypto random
provider the first time the thread draws, so no thread draws the default
sequence; if no provider is loaded yet the clock seeds it provisionally, and
a provider, once available, replaces that on the next draw. An explicit seed
(picoquic_public_random_seed_64) defines the thread's sequence instead: it
mixes into the current state (reset first restores the known one), consumes
no crypto-provider entropy, and is final, so a later draw does not reseed
over it. picoquic_public_random_seed() still mixes a context's crypto random
into the calling thread's state. The explicitly seeded deterministic sequences the tests rely on are
unchanged; an unseeded first draw now seeds from the crypto provider (or,
with none loaded, provisionally from the clock) rather than the default
array, as above.

Test: public_random_thread (util_test.c) pins the reset sequence to a known
answer and checks it is reproducible; that a reseed on another thread leaves
this thread's sequence on its exact continuation (it used to restart it);
that the same seed with reset gives the same sequence on any thread, and two
threads that never seed draw different sequences; that two threads drawing in
lockstep, and a reseed on one overlapping the other's draws, each keep their
own sequence; and, through a counting crypto provider, that an explicit seed
pulls no provider entropy and is deterministic while a provider-less first
draw is provisional and a later provider upgrades it exactly once. A
half-created worker pair settles through a barrier abort rather than hanging.

